# Population Maps Project Rules

## Project Structure
- Monorepo with backend and frontend workspaces
- Backend: Express.js API serving population data
- Frontend: React + Vite with interactive maps
- Data: World Bank CSV in /data directory

## Coding Standards

### General
- Use ES modules (import/export)
- Use async/await for async operations
- Handle errors gracefully with try/catch
- Keep functions small and focused
- Add comments for complex logic

### Backend
- Keep routes in server.js
- Business logic in services/
- Use descriptive endpoint names
- Always validate input
- Cache expensive operations
- Use proper HTTP status codes

### Frontend
- Functional components with hooks
- Props validation where needed
- Keep components under 200 lines
- Separate logic from presentation
- Use semantic HTML
- Maintain accessibility

### CSS
- Component-specific CSS in component folder
- Use meaningful class names
- Follow BEM naming when applicable
- Keep specificity low
- Mobile-first responsive design
- Use CSS variables for repeated values

## File Organization
- Components in frontend/src/components/
- Each component has .jsx and .css file
- Services in backend/src/services/
- Shared utilities in separate files

## Git Workflow
- Write clear commit messages
- Keep commits atomic and focused
- Don't commit node_modules or .env
- Use .gitignore properly

## Performance
- Backend: Cache processed data
- Frontend: Minimize re-renders
- Use React.memo for heavy components
- Lazy load when appropriate

## Dependencies
- Minimal dependencies
- Use standard libraries when possible
- Keep packages updated
- Document why each package is needed

## Testing
- Test API endpoints manually with curl
- Test frontend in browser
- Check responsive design
- Verify error states

## Documentation
- README.md for overview
- DEVELOPMENT.md for dev guide
- Comments for non-obvious code
- Update docs when adding features
